    {
      "source": "gcc/final_permit.pdf",
      "locator": "page 6, section part_i",
      "exact_span": "A method is \"sufficiently sensitive\" when (1) the method minimum level (ML) of quantification is at or below the level of the applicable effluent limit for the measured pollutant or pollutant parameter; or (2) if there is no EPA-approved analytical method with a published ML at or below the effluent limit (see table below), then the method has the lowest published ML (is the most sensitive) of the analytical methods approved under 40 CFR Part 136 or required under 40 CFR Chapter I, Subchapters N or 0, for the measured pollutant or pollutant parameter; or (3) the method is specified in this permit or has been otherwise approved in writing by the permitting authority (EPA Region 6) for the measured pollutant or pollutant parameter.",
      "affected_purpose": "A,B,C",
      "computation_change_reason": "Which of the three alternative sensitivity criteria applies to a given pollutant and method determines the operative quantification threshold. A different reading of criterion (1) versus (2) versus (3), or of what counts as the 'applicable effluent limit' or 'lowest published ML,' changes whether a reported value can be compared to an enforceable limit (A), whether monitoring with a given method satisfies permit conditions (B), and whether a missing, non-detect, or below-ML result is treated as documented no-data versus inadequate evidence (C)."
    },
    {
      "source": "gcc/final_permit.pdf",
      "locator": "page 6, section part_i",
      "exact_span": "The Permittee has the option of developing and submitting a report to justify the use of matrix or sample-specific MLs rather than the published levels. Upon written approval by EPA Region 6 the matrix or sample-specific MLs may be utilized by the Permittee for all future Discharge Monitoring Report (DMR) reporting requirements.",
      "affected_purpose": "A,B,C",
      "computation_change_reason": "If matrix- or sample-specific MLs have been approved in writing, the effective quantification level for DMR reporting differs from published levels. Interpretation of whether such approval exists, and whether it governs 'all future' DMR reporting for a parameter, changes limit-comparison applicability and exceedance findings (A), whether monitoring met method-sensitivity obligations (B), and the semantics of reported or missing values relative to an approved ML (C)."
    },
    {
      "source": "gcc/final_permit.pdf",
      "locator": "page 6, section part_i",
      "exact_span": "Current EPA Region 6 minimum quantification levels (MQLs) for reporting and compliance are provided in Appendix A of Part II of this permit.",
      "affected_purpose": "A,B,C",
      "computation_change_reason": "This cross-reference ties Appendix A MQL values to both reporting and compliance. Interpretation of whether Appendix A values define the compliance comparison threshold, merely reporting conventions, or both affects which numeric limit applies for comparison (A), whether monitoring must meet Appendix A MQLs to satisfy permit conditions (B), and how sub-MQL or absent results are categorized when Appendix A is invoked (C)."
    },
    {
      "source": "gcc/final_permit.pdf",
      "locator": "page 6, section part_i, table",
      "exact_span": "The following pollutants may not have EPA approved methods with a published ML at or below the effluent limit, if specified:",
